To return the IDs of the three highest scores from an array of objects in JavaScript, you can use the following approach:

```javascript
function getTopThreeScores(scores) {
    // Sort the scores in descending order to get the largest values first
    const sortedScores = scores.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);

    // Get the top three scores
    const topThreeScores = sortedScores.slice(0, 3);

    // Return the IDs of these scores
    return topThreeScores.map(score => score.id);
}

// Example usage:
const scores = [
    { id: 1, score: 95 },
    { id: 2, score: 85 },
    { id: 3, score: 75 },
    { id: 4, score: 65 }
];

console.log(getTopThreeScores(scores)); // Output: [4, 2, 1]
```

In this example, we have an array of objects where each object has properties `id` and `score`. We sort the scores in descending order based on their `score` property. Then, we slice the array to only include the top three scores and map over them to extract the `id` values.

This code will work for any number of scores as long as there are at least three scores available. If there are fewer than three scores, it will still return the correct result.
